narrative:
Talbot farm looks smaller after the Salt Road. The fence is still crooked. The same pear tree leans over the well as though listening for gossip. But there is smoke from the chimney now, and laundry snapping on a line, and a boy in patched boots trying to teach a one-winged hen to come when called.

Mira is splitting kindling behind the shed. She sees you before you speak. For a moment, the axe stays raised above her shoulder. Then she lets it down.

@Mira: You came back. I had a speech prepared for if you did. It was better yesterday.

Her little brother, Tovin, runs up with a wooden sword and stops short when he recognizes you.

@Tovin: Mira said you were the kind of person who makes trouble smaller.
@Mira: I said no such thing. I said they make it different.

The correction is dry, but her hand rests briefly on Tovin's head. It is the closest thing to a welcome she knows how to offer.

Inside, the kitchen is warm enough to fog the window. Mira pours barley soup into three bowls and gives Tovin the fullest one without looking at it. The habit is practiced; so is the guilt that follows it.

@Mira: The road got its speeches. We got a new assessor. Same boots, different seal. He says the levy will be fairer after the winter.
@You: Do you believe him?
@Mira: I believe he has never watched a child count bread. That is not the same thing.

She sets your bowl down, then sits across from you. There is a pause long enough for the fire to settle.

@Mira: I used to think surviving was the whole job. Then you came through here and made surviving feel like a question with witnesses. I am still deciding whether I am grateful for that.

Tovin pushes his wooden sword across the table toward you. The blade has been carved from a broken chair rung.

@Tovin: It is for fighting officials. Mira says that is not what it is for.
@Mira: Mira says it is for learning that a weapon is heavier after you use it.

The next evening, six farmers meet in the Talbot barn. They bring stools, suspicion, and figures scratched on scraps of feed paper. Mira stands near the door at first, listening to everyone tell her why cooperation will fail.

Then the new assessor arrives with two guards and a ledger tucked under his arm.

@Assessor: Unlicensed meetings about grain distribution can be mistaken for conspiracy.

Mira steps forward before anyone else can. Her hands are shaking. She does not hide them.

@Mira: Then write this down carefully. We are conspiring to keep our children alive through winter.

The guard reaches for her elbow. You move between them. Your hand goes to the hilt of your sword, but you do not draw it. The barn goes quiet enough to hear rain striking the roof.

@You: Touch her, and every person here will remember your name.

The assessor studies the room. He has a ledger; Mira has six people who have stopped looking at the floor.

When you leave, Mira walks with you to the pear tree. She does not ask you to stay. That would make the moment easier, and neither of you seems interested in easy things.

@Mira: Tovin thinks you are coming back.
@You: Do you?

She looks toward the field, where the first green blades are beginning to take hold.

@Mira: I think you have a habit of appearing when a person has run out of lies. It is inconvenient.

Then she smiles—small, real, and gone almost at once.

@Mira: Ride safe. And if you come back, bring news worth hearing. Not promises.

The road north waits beyond the gate. Behind you, the farm is no longer only a place that survived. It is a place that has begun to speak for itself.

You recognize Talbot farm before you see it: the pear tree bent over the well, the fence post Mira once swore at until it seemed personally ashamed. This time the gate is shut.

Mira is standing in the yard with a sack of feed at her feet. She sees you. Neither of you pretends not to.

@Mira: I heard the road had made you important.
@You: Mira—
@Mira: Do not use my name like it is an apology. You had your chance to be right. You chose to be certain.

Tovin watches from the doorway, old enough now to understand that adults can hurt each other without raising a hand.

You could explain the hunger, the law, the fear that brought you to your earlier choice. None of it would make the gate open.

Mira does not answer immediately. The wind moves through the pear tree and lifts a loose strand of hair across her face.

@Mira: Go north. There are larger things waiting for you. There always are.

She picks up the feed sack and carries it inside. The gate remains shut. Whatever may heal here will need more than one road, more than one speech, and more time than either of you has offered yet.
